Ask someone to picture "3D on the web" and they'll usually imagine a chunky polygon model spinning on a white background. Useful, sometimes. Immersive, rarely.

3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) is a different kind of thing. Instead of building a surface out of triangles, it represents a scene as millions of tiny, soft, colored points — splats — each holding position, color, and how it catches the light. Render enough of them and you don't get a model of a place. You get the place.

## Why it matters for living worlds

The point of a living, immersive world is that you forget the rendering entirely. You're not admiring geometry; you're somewhere. Splats get you there faster than almost anything else because:

- **Capture is cheap.** A phone video or a set of photos is enough to reconstruct a real space.
- **It runs in the browser.** WebGPU makes real-time splat rendering viable on commodity hardware — no install, no plugin.
- **It reads as real.** Soft edges, real light, real depth. It sidesteps the uncanny flatness of low-poly scenes.

## Not a demo — a surface

The trick isn't rendering a splat scene once. It's making it a surface you can build on: put people in it, wire it to live inputs, let it react and remember. A captured room is the starting canvas, not the finished piece.

